-- title:
--   Lemma 5.3 — Lyapunov drift lemma (corrected: limsup/liminf in the extended reals)
-- statement:
--   Let $U(t)\in\mathbb R^N$ be a backlog process on a probability space, $L\ge 0$ a Lyapunov function with $\mathbb E\,L(U(t))<\infty$ for all $t$, and $\Delta(U(t)):=\mathbb E\{L(U(t+1))-L(U(t))\mid U(t)\}$ the one-step drift (conditioning on the $\sigma$-algebra generated by $U(t)$). Suppose there are integrable scalar processes $x(t),y(t)$ such that for every slot $t$
--   $$\Delta(U(t))\le\mathbb E\{y(t)\mid U(t)\}-\mathbb E\{x(t)\mid U(t)\}\qquad\text{a.s.}\tag{5.17}$$
--   Then, in the extended reals,
--   $$\limsup_{t\to\infty}\frac1t\sum_{\tau=0}^{t-1}\mathbb E\,x(\tau)\le\limsup_{t\to\infty}\frac1t\sum_{\tau=0}^{t-1}\mathbb E\,y(\tau),$$
--   and the same inequality holds with $\liminf$ in place of $\limsup$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The retired statement took both sides as `Filter.limsup` on $\mathbb R$, which returns the junk value $0$ for a sequence that is unbounded above; a $y$-average tending to $+\infty$ was therefore compared as if it were $0$ and the inequality failed (the accepted disproof). The corrected statement casts the Cesàro averages into `EReal`, where $\limsup$ and $\liminf$ always exist and the inequality is trivially true when the right-hand side is $+\infty$ — exactly the reading intended by the monograph. The hypothesis $\mathbb E\,L(U(0))<\infty$ is the monograph's; integrability of $L(U(t))$ at every $t$ is stated so that the drift (a conditional expectation) is not a junk value.
-- source:
--   Georgiadis, Neely & Tassiulas, Resource Allocation and Cross-Layer Control in Wireless Networks, Foundations and Trends in Networking 1(1), 2006, p. 81, Lemma 5.3 (Lyapunov Drift), with the drift definition ∆(U(t)) of p. 81

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_NetworkControl_UtilityFairness_drift

namespace NetworkControl.UtilityFairness

open MeasureTheory

/-- Lemma 5.3 (Lyapunov Drift), Georgiadis–Neely–Tassiulas p. 81. Let `L ≥ 0` be a Lyapunov
function of the backlog vector with `E L(U(0)) < ∞`. If there are scalar processes `x(t), y(t)`
such that for every slot `t` the drift satisfies `∆(U(t)) ≤ E{y(t) | U(t)} - E{x(t) | U(t)}`
(5.17), then
`limsup_t (1/t) Σ_{τ<t} E x(τ) ≤ limsup_t (1/t) Σ_{τ<t} E y(τ)`, and likewise for `liminf`.

Corrected version: both sides are read in the extended reals `EReal`, exactly as the monograph
intends (the inequality is trivially true when the `y`-averages diverge to `+∞`). The retired
version used `Filter.limsup` on `ℝ`, which returns the junk value `0` for a sequence unbounded
above, so a `y`-average tending to `+∞` was compared as if it were `0`. -/
theorem lemma_lyapunov_drift_v2
    {Ω : Type*} [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω] {P : Measure Ω} [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    {N : ℕ} (L : (Fin N → ℝ) → ℝ) (hLnn : ∀ u, 0 ≤ L u)
    (U : ℕ → Ω → Fin N → ℝ) (x y : ℕ → Ω → ℝ)
    (hMeasU : ∀ t : ℕ, ∀ i : Fin N, Measurable (fun ω => U t ω i))
    (hIntegL : ∀ t : ℕ, Integrable (fun ω => L (U t ω)) P)
    (hIntegX : ∀ t : ℕ, Integrable (x t) P)
    (hIntegY : ∀ t : ℕ, Integrable (y t) P)
    (hdrift : ∀ t : ℕ,
      drift P L U t
        ≤ᵐ[P] ((P[y t | MeasurableSpace.comap (U t) inferInstance])
                - (P[x t | MeasurableSpace.comap (U t) inferInstance]))) :
    (Filter.limsup
        (fun t : ℕ => (((1 / (t : ℝ)) * ∑ τ ∈ Finset.range t, ∫ ω, x τ ω ∂P : ℝ) : EReal))
        Filter.atTop
      ≤ Filter.limsup
        (fun t : ℕ => (((1 / (t : ℝ)) * ∑ τ ∈ Finset.range t, ∫ ω, y τ ω ∂P : ℝ) : EReal))
        Filter.atTop) ∧
    (Filter.liminf
        (fun t : ℕ => (((1 / (t : ℝ)) * ∑ τ ∈ Finset.range t, ∫ ω, x τ ω ∂P : ℝ) : EReal))
        Filter.atTop
      ≤ Filter.liminf
        (fun t : ℕ => (((1 / (t : ℝ)) * ∑ τ ∈ Finset.range t, ∫ ω, y τ ω ∂P : ℝ) : EReal))
        Filter.atTop) := by sorry

end NetworkControl.UtilityFairness
